Feature: spa-logged-in
Target module: SPAEngine

- SPAEngine.loggedIn: public boolean property (default false; TypeError for non-booleans).
  isLoggedIn(), which protected routes call on first load and on SPA link clicks, now
  returns it. The app owns the value: it sets it before initialize() on every page load
  (it is held in memory, so a reload resets it), true after sign-in, false on sign-out.
- Fix: isLoggedIn() had no return statement, so it returned undefined and every protected
  route redirected, signed in or not. It also read sessionStorage.userProperties.auth, a
  property of a string. Protected routes could not be used before this change.
- Fix: the redirect for a protected route read routeProperties.authURL instead of
  routeProperties.properties.authURL, sending signed-out users to /undefined (SPA link
  click and first load). The default-route branch already read it correctly.

Why: app.myinvitees.com needed a protected /card-writer route and was working around the
broken check in its own page code.

Verified: headless Chromium against app.myinvitees.com (dev-server.js + its backend):
signed out -> /card-writer and the home page's SPA link both land on /login; sign-up lands
on /card-writer; a reload while signed in stays there (flag restored from the token before
initialize()); the SPA link while signed in renders the tool; sign-out and a later visit go
to /login; a forged token reaches the page, the backend rejects it and the app signs out.
No page errors or dialogs. No examples/spa change: it has no protected routes.

Affected projects: consumers of vUX-spaEngine.js (app.myinvitees.com, vGet). No API
removed. Apps with protected: true routes now need to set spa.loggedIn; before this change
those routes always redirected, so no working app depended on the old behaviour.
